schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Is Your Community Ready for a Local Node?
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A readiness check for organizers standing up a local ReFi node, arguing
    that the technology is the easy part and the gap between imagined and
    actual participation is what kills these projects. Uses the nine-stage
    Community Readiness Model to set "stage 5, preparation" as the honest
    launch cutoff, lists green/red flags, gives a three-part readiness test
    (organize one small thing, handle one small conflict, sustain effort
    three months), and warns against single-founder dependency using the
    collapse of Ithaca Hours, the Bristol Pound, and the Brixton Pound as
    cautionary examples, contrasted with the resilient, multi-stakeholder
    Sarafu Network.
  audience: organizers preparing to launch a local ReFi node/chapter
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      this-isnt-gatekeeping-framing-vs-the-real-effect-of-telling-a-community-it-isnt-ready-yet
  related_concepts:
    - community-readiness-model-nine-stage-readiness-assessment
  related_resources:
    - tri-ethnic-center-community-readiness-model-assessment-manuals
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/is-community-ready.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  notes: >-
    assessed-but-excluded: "Grassroots Economics / Sarafu Network" is not
    re-created as a new resource object — it already exists in
    data/kb/resource.yaml from a prior ingestion slice. A separate
    "single point of failure" concept-lineage was not created — the
    framework already has an equivalent "Bus factor" concept-lineage; the
    Ithaca Hours/Bristol Pound/Brixton Pound specifics are instead captured
    as a claim-evidence object linked to that existing concept.
